{"candidates":[{"content":"The 'Synchronization Resonance' failure mode in large services (SRC_005) integrates with the 'Redundancy Paradox' (SRC_004) by providing the specific physical mechanism—systemic oscillations—through which the increased complexity of the synchronization layer manifests as a correlated failure. This relation is refuted if 'Synchronization Resonance' can occur in decentralized networks without a global synchronization layer.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"84e5ea7f8390d4b391f2d7d2c2f4e57ccae2b1ed621ae9cc06c1fb1d119b7673"},{"role":"dependence","target":"0147cbffd74ee0492d669015f2968e5d21045c0ab9631a2a3b66d604b8bc1480"}],"typicality":0.9},{"content":"The claim that large services are more resilient due to efficient 'circuit breaking' (SRC_001) contradicts the 'Redundancy Paradox' (SRC_002), as the latter posits that the very coordination layer required for such global management becomes the primary single point of failure. This relation is refuted if a coordination layer can be implemented that provides global load shedding without introducing a systemic single point of failure.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"5256fc7a4ff5c37de5c351521bb5c356f0a7f62b676cbe11a5a58af56c46acad"},{"role":"dependence","target":"5706c9666ec74b13c87130f0815c3e45953f719be12146a1c27c49eb69137350"}],"typicality":0.85},{"content":"The 'Redundancy Paradox' (SRC_003) abstracts the specific failure modes of 'Synchronization Resonance' (SRC_005) and 'Coordination Complexity' (SRC_004) into a general principle where internal redundancy eventually yields diminishing returns on resilience. This relation is refuted if there exists a form of internal redundancy that does not rely on a coordination layer and does not trigger resonance.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"73c77c36a70e22910bc14f706c0472fc290adbc063c5d50b81b0eb15c58d78e0"},{"role":"dependence","target":"84e5ea7f8390d4b391f2d7d2c2f4e57ccae2b1ed621ae9cc06c1fb1d119b7673"},{"role":"dependence","target":"0147cbffd74ee0492d669015f2968e5d21045c0ab9631a2a3b66d604b8bc1480"}],"typicality":0.7},{"content":"The resilience of decentralized networks against 'systemic oscillations' (SRC_005) depends on the absence of tight coupling, which is the same mechanism that makes them prone to 'cascading failures' via inter-service dependencies (SRC_001). This relation is refuted if a network can be simultaneously loosely coupled enough to avoid resonance but tightly dependent enough to suffer cascading timeouts.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"5256fc7a4ff5c37de5c351521bb5c356f0a7f62b676cbe11a5a58af56c46acad"},{"role":"dependence","target":"0147cbffd74ee0492d669015f2968e5d21045c0ab9631a2a3b66d604b8bc1480"}],"typicality":0.6},{"content":"The 'Redundancy Paradox' (SRC_002) reduces to a problem of 'Coordination Layer Complexity' (SRC_004), where the failure probability of the consensus algorithm grows faster than the reliability gain from adding redundant nodes. This relation is refuted if the complexity of the coordination layer remains constant regardless of the number of redundant nodes added.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"5706c9666ec74b13c87130f0815c3e45953f719be12146a1c27c49eb69137350"},{"role":"dependence","target":"84e5ea7f8390d4b391f2d7d2c2f4e57ccae2b1ed621ae9cc06c1fb1d119b7673"}],"typicality":0.8}],"simulation_proposals":[]}